//! Diodes.
//!
//! Library parts state only what is always true of them; a design narrows the rest. The
//! pads are KiCad's convention for a diode (cathode 1, anode 2), as for `Led`. A diode is
//! not a symmetric part, so the checker gives it the voltage of a rail it sits across with
//! its sign, `.bridge_voltage`, and its `.bias`, the voltage it blocks, follows from that:
//! a diode wired the wrong way round across a rail is found (ADR 0088, see `Diode`).

/// A rectifier or switching diode. Current flows from anode to cathode.
///
/// `.bias` is the voltage across the diode in the direction it blocks: the voltage at the
/// cathode relative to the anode, so it is negative when the diode conducts. A diode placed
/// between a rail's `pos` and `neg` takes it from that rail (ADR 0013, ADR 0088): the rail's
/// voltage when the cathode is on `pos` and its negative when the anode is, so a diode wired
/// the wrong way round across a rail has a negative bias and `.bias >= -.forward_voltage`
/// fails, naming it. A design states `.bias` for a diode that is across no
/// rail, such as a reverse-polarity diode on an input (the voltage it must block is the one
/// the input can be wired to).
///
/// `.bridge_voltage` is the voltage at the anode relative to the cathode, the first end of
/// the bridge relative to the second: the checker derives it from the rail the diode is
/// across, with the sign of the way it is wired. It is `optional` (ADR 0074): a diode that is
/// across no rail has none, and the relations that read it are then neither checked nor listed
/// as not evaluated.
///
/// A rating is a bound the datasheet gives, so each is checked in the worst case:
/// `.current <= .max_forward_current` and `.bias <= .max_reverse_voltage`. A rating the design
/// does not reach (a current it does not state) is listed as not evaluated.
///
/// Why the voltage has a sign (ADR 0088). The checker gave every part with a `bridge` and a
/// `.bias` the rail's voltage whichever end of the part was on `pos`, as a ceramic capacitor
/// wants, so a rectifier with its anode on `pos` was given the rail's voltage as the voltage
/// it blocks, and passed: it conducts across the rail, a short. A part that has a `bridge`
/// and a voltage field named `.bridge_voltage` is given the voltage from the first end of its
/// bridge to the second instead (one relation, `part.bridge_voltage == rail.voltage` or
/// `== -rail.voltage`, with the same choice of rail as `.bias`), and is not given `.bias` by
/// that code: its own relations define the bias from it. It is a field name the checker looks
/// for, as ADR 0086 says, and a project's own diode part copied from this one has to add it.
/// `.bias >= -.forward_voltage` then says the cathode is never more than a forward drop below
/// the anode, and fails, naming the diode, for one wired to conduct across a rail. A diode in
/// series, across no rail, has nothing derived and the board states `.bias`; `.bridge_voltage`
/// is `optional` so that the relations reading it are dropped for it, with no note (a first
/// version without `optional` listed them as not evaluated for every diode in series). The
/// orientation check of a bare `Diode` compares with `.forward_voltage`, which a `Schottky`
/// has from its catalog row and a bare `Diode` only when the design states it; without it a
/// reversed one is not found. Not taken: a signed `.bias` for every part (a capacitor's would
/// go negative on a reversed rail, and a bridge direction does not say which end is the
/// cathode: the bridge of a `Diode` is the way it conducts, of a `Zener` the way it clamps);
/// the cathode from a pin's name or a marker in the `pins` grammar (a name is a convention in
/// the checker, a marker is a language change); syntax for the field; a checker error with its
/// own wording for a reversed diode (the relation already says it in numbers); `.drop` as the
/// voltage (the DC analysis reads it as the forward voltage, so deriving it from the rail
/// would contradict the analysis for every blocking diode). A diode between two rails (an
/// OR-ing diode) is across no rail and is not checked. The name, and the field-name convention
/// (ADR 0105), are accepted as built (the owner, 2026-11-11).
part Diode {
    .designator &= "D"

    pins {
        .anode   passive "A" "2"
        .cathode passive "K" "1"
    }

    .forward_voltage: Voltage
    /// The largest voltage the diode blocks (repetitive peak reverse voltage).
    .max_reverse_voltage: Voltage
    /// The largest continuous (average) forward current.
    .max_forward_current: Current
    /// Through-current, anode to cathode.
    .current: Current
    /// Voltage at the anode relative to the cathode.
    .drop: Voltage
    /// The DC voltage across the diode in the direction it blocks, cathode relative to anode:
    /// negative when it conducts.
    .bias: Voltage
    /// The voltage at the anode relative to the cathode. A diode across a rail takes it from
    /// the rail, the way round it is wired.
    .bridge_voltage: Voltage optional
    /// The most current that can reach the diode through the part in front of it in its `~>`
    /// chain that limits it (a fuse's rating), which the checker gives it (ADR 0091). A diode
    /// with no such part in front of it has none, and the relation is left out.
    .supply_fault_current: Current optional
    /// The highest voltage the supply of the `~>` chain the diode is first in (to have one) reaches the
    /// wrong way round: the `.reverse_peak` of the rail the chain starts on, as a positive
    /// number, which the checker gives it (ADR 0091). The diode blocks it.
    .supply_reverse_peak: Voltage optional

    .drop == .forward_voltage
    .current <= .max_forward_current
    .bias == -.bridge_voltage
    .bias <= .max_reverse_voltage
    .bias >= -.forward_voltage
    .supply_fault_current <= .max_forward_current
    .supply_reverse_peak <= .max_reverse_voltage
    bridge .anode ~> .cathode
    dc diode .anode .cathode .forward_voltage

    .package: Package
}

/// A Schottky diode: a `Diode` with a low forward voltage and a metal junction. It has the
/// same fields and ratings, and is its own kind because it is a different symbol and a
/// different catalog, and because a design says which it needs ("a Schottky for the
/// reverse-polarity drop").
generic Schottky {
    .designator &= "D"

    pins {
        .anode   passive "A" "2"
        .cathode passive "K" "1"
    }

    .forward_voltage: Voltage
    /// The largest voltage the diode blocks (repetitive peak reverse voltage).
    .max_reverse_voltage: Voltage
    /// The largest continuous (average) forward current.
    .max_forward_current: Current
    /// Through-current, anode to cathode.
    .current: Current
    /// Voltage at the anode relative to the cathode.
    .drop: Voltage
    /// The DC voltage across the diode in the direction it blocks, cathode relative to anode:
    /// negative when it conducts.
    .bias: Voltage
    /// The voltage at the anode relative to the cathode. A diode across a rail takes it from
    /// the rail, the way round it is wired.
    .bridge_voltage: Voltage optional
    /// The most current that can reach the diode through the part in front of it in its `~>`
    /// chain that limits it (a fuse's rating), which the checker gives it (ADR 0091). A diode
    /// with no such part in front of it has none, and the relation is left out.
    .supply_fault_current: Current optional
    /// The highest voltage the supply of the `~>` chain the diode is first in (to have one) reaches the
    /// wrong way round: the `.reverse_peak` of the rail the chain starts on, as a positive
    /// number, which the checker gives it (ADR 0091). The diode blocks it.
    .supply_reverse_peak: Voltage optional

    .drop == .forward_voltage
    .current <= .max_forward_current
    .bias == -.bridge_voltage
    .bias <= .max_reverse_voltage
    .bias >= -.forward_voltage
    .supply_fault_current <= .max_forward_current
    .supply_reverse_peak <= .max_reverse_voltage
    bridge .anode ~> .cathode
    dc diode .anode .cathode .forward_voltage

    .package: Package
    catalog schottky { .forward_voltage, .max_reverse_voltage, .max_forward_current, .package }
}

/// A Zener diode, used in its reverse direction: it conducts from cathode to anode above
/// `.zener_voltage`. The voltage is a range because of the tolerance
/// (`.zener_voltage &= 5.1V +/- 5%`); in a catalog row it is the datasheet's minimum to
/// maximum at the test current `.test_current`.
///
/// A Zener is used two ways, so the part states neither. As a clamp, the rail across it must
/// stay below the breakdown, and the design says so with one line,
/// `.z.bias <= .z.zener_voltage`, which holds only if the highest rail is under the lowest
/// breakdown. As a shunt regulator, it sits across the rail it makes and the rail's voltage
/// is the Zener's. What is always true is the power limit: the voltage across it times the
/// current through it, cathode to anode, is at most `.max_power`.
///
/// What is also always true is that a Zener directly across a rail does not surely conduct:
/// `min(.bias) <= max(.zener_voltage)` (ADR 0098), the least voltage across it is at most the
/// greatest breakdown voltage. A rail that stays above the breakdown in every case puts the
/// part in breakdown all the time, with nothing to limit its current, and is an error. It is
/// the sure case only: a rail range that straddles the breakdown may conduct, and passes. A
/// Zener behind a series resistor has no rail across it, so `.bias` has no value and the
/// rule waits. The rule also chooses among the catalog rows, as any relation of a part does:
/// a design that states no `.zener_voltage` is given a row whose breakdown reaches the rail.
///
/// What a design needs to bound a clamp over temperature is in four more members, each an
/// optional column of the catalog (ADR 0078): a row that does not state one is still offered
/// to a design that does not mention it, and a design that names it is offered only the
/// rows that state it. `.test_current` is the current the datasheet publishes
/// `.zener_voltage` at. `.temperature_coefficient` is the datasheet's range of the change of
/// the voltage for one kelvin, in volts (`33.4mV..41.2mV` for "33.4 to 41.2 mV/C"; it is a
/// `Voltage` because no kind holds volts per kelvin). `.leakage_current` is the reverse current
/// below the breakdown, stated at `.leakage_test_voltage`.
///
/// A design that runs the clamp over a temperature range has `.temperature_shift`, how far the
/// temperature goes from the 25 C of the datasheet (kelvin away from 25 C: 125 C is `100K`,
/// -40 C is `-65K`; a range gives both corners). A board that states an environment
/// (`.environment.temperature`, ADR 0099) does not write it: the checker derives it as the
/// environment's temperature less 25 C, so `-40degC..125degC` is `-65K..100K`, and the
/// shift is the row's `.temperature_coefficient` applied over that range. A board that states
/// `.temperature_shift` itself, `&= -65K..100K`, overrides the derivation. The checker derives
/// it only for a board that bounds `.voltage_over_temperature` (`.z.voltage_over_temperature
/// <= 45V`), since the shift asks every row for its coefficient: a board that does not bound
/// it keeps the rows that state none, as it did before. A board with no environment and no
/// stated shift has no such relation, so it is not listed as not evaluated, and the row's
/// temperature coefficient is not asked for. `.voltage_over_temperature` is then
/// `.zener_voltage + .temperature_coefficient * .temperature_shift`, in the worst case: the
/// upper end of the range is the voltage at the hot corner and the lower end the voltage at
/// the cold one, so `.z.voltage_over_temperature <= 45V` is the check of a clamp against a
/// limit. The environment's range is used as it stands: one that does not hold 25 C (`50degC..
/// 80degC` is `25K..55K`) gives the band over that range, not one widened to the datasheet's
/// 25 C. `.voltage_over_temperature` is the datasheet's typical figure carried over the range:
/// a datasheet's coefficient is not a bound on a part (the standard rows state the datasheet's
/// typical coefficient as its minimum to maximum, in `notes`, with the page), and the margin is
/// the design's.
///
/// A Zener is a clamp, not a rectifier, so across a rail `.bias >= 0V` says it is not forward
/// biased (ADR 0088): it fails for one with its anode on `pos`. It is `0V` and not
/// `-.forward_voltage` because a Zener has no such field, and a datasheet's `VF`, where it
/// exists, would allow a clamp across an AC line. The part's `.bridge_voltage` is the voltage
/// at the cathode relative to the anode, the way it clamps, which is its `.bias`.
///
/// Why these members (ADR 0080, issue #199). A real 12 V automotive board held a supply pin under
/// its absolute maximum with a series resistor and a Zener across it. The voltage range of a
/// Zener is published at a test current and at 25 C, and the board needed it at the hot and the
/// cold end of its temperature range. It stated the test current, the temperature shift and the
/// leakage by hand, with the datasheet page in a comment, and nothing tied them to the row the
/// solver picked: another row would pass in silence with the wrong numbers. `.zener_voltage`
/// already is the datasheet's minimum to maximum of VZ at `.test_current`, so there is no second
/// pair of members for the minimum and the maximum. Each of the seven rows read when this was
/// written states all four columns from its datasheet table, with the table and page in `notes`,
/// or states none (a catalog test requires all four together). Rows beyond them need a person to
/// read their library type on JLCPCB (ADR 0039).
///
/// What was not taken:
///
/// - The coefficient as a `Number` fraction per kelvin, like a resistor's. The datasheet
///   publishes millivolts per kelvin, and a fraction needs a division by a voltage that is itself
///   a range, a figure the datasheet does not give. A `Voltage` per kelvin keeps its number and
///   its unit. No kind holds volts per kelvin, and a new quantity kind is a language choice.
/// - `.temperature_coefficient` marked `optional` as a member (ADR 0074). A value a catalog row
///   gives is never a statement, so the relation would be skipped for every design that does not
///   state the coefficient by hand, and the row's figure would be ignored: the opposite of the
///   issue. The member marker guards what a design states (the shift); the column marker (ADR
///   0078) lets a row be empty.
/// - `.temperature_shift` as a plain `Number`: `Temperature` exists (kelvin, a difference), and
///   the relation divides by `1K` to reach a plain count.
/// - Absolute temperatures, with 25 C in the library: there is no absolute-temperature literal,
///   and a shift from the datasheet's 25 C is what the datasheet states.
/// - A separate hot and cold member: one range carries both corners, and a design asks for
///   either end with `<=` or `>=`.
///
/// Limits. The coefficient is a typical figure, not a bound on a part, so the corners are
/// typical-based and the margin is the design's. The test current of the coefficient is `IZTC`,
/// which equals IZT in every row filled; a datasheet where they differ needs another member. The
/// voltage is the 25 C range at the test current; the dynamic impedance at another current is not
/// modelled.
///
/// The unit of the coefficient, the name and kind of the shift, which marker guards what, and the
/// relation's form are accepted as built by the owner, 2026-11-11.
generic Zener {
    .designator &= "D"

    pins {
        .anode   passive "A" "2"
        .cathode passive "K" "1"
    }

    /// The breakdown voltage, with its tolerance.
    .zener_voltage: Voltage
    .max_power: Power
    /// The current `.zener_voltage` is published at (IZT).
    .test_current: Current
    /// The change of `.zener_voltage` for one kelvin, as a range of volts.
    .temperature_coefficient: Voltage
    /// The reverse current below the breakdown, at `.leakage_test_voltage`.
    .leakage_current: Current
    /// The reverse voltage `.leakage_current` is stated at.
    .leakage_test_voltage: Voltage
    /// How far the temperature goes from 25 C, in kelvin. A design states it to have
    /// `.voltage_over_temperature`.
    .temperature_shift: Temperature optional
    /// `.zener_voltage` over `.temperature_shift`, in the worst case.
    .voltage_over_temperature: Voltage
    /// Through-current in the breakdown direction, cathode to anode.
    .current: Current
    /// The DC voltage across the diode, cathode relative to anode. A Zener placed between a
    /// rail's `pos` and `neg` takes it from that rail, with the sign of the way it is wired.
    .bias: Voltage
    /// The voltage from the cathode to the anode: the same as `.bias`, as it is the bridge's.
    .bridge_voltage: Voltage optional

    .bias == .bridge_voltage
    .bias >= 0V
    min(.bias) <= max(.zener_voltage)
    .bias * .current <= .max_power
    .voltage_over_temperature == .zener_voltage + .temperature_coefficient * (.temperature_shift / 1K)
    bridge .cathode ~> .anode

    .package: Package
    catalog zener {
        .zener_voltage, .max_power, .package,
        .test_current optional, .temperature_coefficient optional,
        .leakage_current optional, .leakage_test_voltage optional
    }
}

/// A unidirectional transient-voltage-suppression diode. It does not conduct below
/// `.standoff_voltage` and clamps to `.clamping_voltage` when the current through it is the
/// peak pulse current `.peak_pulse_current`. Cathode to the protected line, anode to ground.
///
/// What is always true is that the rail across it stays below the stand-off, or it would
/// conduct in normal use: `.bias <= .standoff_voltage`, with `.bias` taken from the rail it
/// is across. What it protects is the board's to say. A design states the lowest absolute
/// maximum of what is behind the diode as `.protected_limit` (a voltage, from that part's
/// datasheet), and then the clamping voltage is checked against it.
///
/// `.breakdown_voltage` is the minimum breakdown voltage VBR, an optional catalog column (ADR
/// 0073; the `catalog` statement marks it `optional`, ADR 0078): between the stand-off voltage
/// and it the diode leaks a little, below it the diode does not conduct. A design that needs
/// a margin from the highest the line reaches states it, `.d.breakdown_voltage >= .vin.peak *
/// 1.1`, and the standard rows, which record no VBR, are then not offered; a project's catalog
/// gives rows that do.
///
/// Across a rail, `.bias >= 0V` says that a clamp is not forward biased (ADR 0088), as for a
/// `Zener`: it fails for one with its anode on `pos`. A `Tvs` first in a `~>` chain from a rail
/// that states a `.reverse_peak` is given it as `.supply_reverse_peak` and states
/// `-.supply_reverse_peak >= 0V`: a unidirectional TVS diode there conducts the reversed
/// supply, and the finding says so with the numbers (ADR 0091). The check reads the rail's
/// `.reverse_peak` as a voltage the diode must not be exposed to, which is the reversed
/// battery of a miswired harness, a steady voltage that turns the diode into a short. A
/// negative transient is clamped by the same diode at about a forward drop, which is its job,
/// and the source limits what it carries. `Rail` has one number for both, so a design that
/// wants the transient and not the reversal states no `.reverse_peak` on that rail; two
/// members, a steady `.reverse_voltage` and a transient `.reverse_peak`, would be a second
/// member on every rail, and are not built. `TvsBidirectional` is not given the field: it
/// clamps a reverse transient as a forward one, so nothing is checked (accepted as built, 2026-11-11).
///
/// Why `.breakdown_voltage` is an optional column (ADR 0073). A design could not say that a TVS
/// diode must stay non-conducting with a margin above the highest its line reaches, because the
/// part had the stand-off and the clamping voltage and no breakdown voltage. A real 12 V
/// automotive board's input protection needed `.clamp.breakdown_voltage >= .vin.peak * 1.1` and
/// carried the column in a project part whose column was required. A file of the `tvs` catalog
/// may leave the column out of its first line, and a row may leave its cell empty, so a
/// project's file written before the column stays valid. A design that does not mention the
/// field is offered every row and has no new check. A design that states it is offered only the
/// rows that record one, as for `.dielectric` and `.rms_current`: a row with an empty cell fits no
/// such design (ADR 0047, ADR 0058). The standard `tvs.csv` states a VBR for the one row whose
/// datasheet table was read (`data/jlcpcb/README.md`), because a value enters a catalog only with
/// its source, so a design that states `.breakdown_voltage` is offered that row and no other
/// standard one, and gets the usual "the `tvs` catalog has nothing for" otherwise, until a
/// project's catalog gives rows with the column. The record does not say at which test current
/// the minimum VBR is given; that goes in a row's `notes`.
///
/// What was not taken:
///
/// - A required column, as the project part had it. Every standard row would need a number with
///   no recorded source, or a design that never mentions VBR would be refused every row. The
///   optional column is what ADR 0055 did for the same reason.
/// - A relation in `Tvs`, such as `.standoff_voltage <= .breakdown_voltage`. It is always true of
///   a real part, but the solver counts a field as asked for as soon as a relation mentions it
///   (`asked`, `crates/netsmith-solve/src/solve.rs`), so it would make every standard row unfit
///   for every TVS diode. ADR 0055 refused the same relation for the inductor's heating current.
/// - A separate generic or part for the diode with a breakdown column: two spellings of one
///   thing, a design that needs the margin would have to know to pick it, and a project would
///   copy the whole part, as a real project did.
/// - Numbers from memory or from the JLCPCB strings in `notes`: they are not read from a
///   datasheet table with a stated condition (VBR is given at a test current, minimum and
///   maximum), and nothing is entered without it.
///
/// Cost. The check is the design's own: the library does not say which margin is enough, and a
/// design with a TVS diode and no `.breakdown_voltage` line has no margin check.
///
/// The written record, the rejected alternatives and the placement of the optional-column rule are
/// accepted as built by the owner, 2026-11-11.
generic Tvs {
    .designator &= "D"

    pins {
        .anode   passive "A" "2"
        .cathode passive "K" "1"
    }

    /// The reverse stand-off (working) voltage: the most the line may carry without the
    /// diode conducting.
    .standoff_voltage: Voltage
    /// The voltage across the diode while it carries `.peak_pulse_current`.
    .clamping_voltage: Voltage
    .peak_pulse_current: Current
    /// The minimum breakdown voltage VBR, unset until a design states it or a catalog row does.
    /// Between `.standoff_voltage` and this the diode leaks a little; below it, it does not
    /// conduct.
    .breakdown_voltage: Voltage
    /// The absolute maximum voltage of the part this one protects. A design states it.
    .protected_limit: Voltage
    /// The DC voltage across the diode, cathode relative to anode. A TVS diode placed between a
    /// rail's `pos` and `neg` takes it from that rail, with the sign of the way it is wired.
    .bias: Voltage
    /// The voltage from the cathode to the anode: the same as `.bias`, as it is the bridge's.
    .bridge_voltage: Voltage optional
    /// The highest voltage the supply of the `~>` chain the diode is first in (to have one)
    /// reaches the wrong way round, as a positive number, which the checker gives it (ADR
    /// 0091). A unidirectional TVS diode conducts when it is reversed: the voltage across it
    /// would be `-.supply_reverse_peak`, which `-.supply_reverse_peak >= 0V` refuses.
    .supply_reverse_peak: Voltage optional

    .bias == .bridge_voltage
    .bias >= 0V
    .bias <= .standoff_voltage
    -.supply_reverse_peak >= 0V
    .clamping_voltage <= .protected_limit
    bridge .cathode ~> .anode

    .package: Package
    catalog tvs { .standoff_voltage, .clamping_voltage, .peak_pulse_current, .package, .breakdown_voltage optional }
}

/// A bidirectional TVS diode: two avalanche junctions back to back, so it stands off the same
/// voltage and clamps to the same voltage whichever way round it is wired, and has no anode
/// and cathode. The fields are those of `Tvs` and mean the same, read for either polarity
/// (ADR 0089). It is a TVS diode for a line that swings both ways or that may be wired
/// backwards (a power input, a CAN or LIN bus line); a line that only goes positive wants the
/// lower clamp of the unidirectional `Tvs`.
///
/// What is always true is that the rail across it stays below the stand-off, or it would
/// conduct in normal use: `.bias <= .standoff_voltage`, with `.bias` taken from the rail it
/// is across. The part is the same either way round, so the checker gives it the rail's
/// voltage whichever pin is on `pos`, as for a capacitor (ADR 0013); a design states `.bias`
/// for one across no rail as the largest voltage the line can reach in either direction. What
/// it protects is the board's to say: `.protected_limit`, the lowest absolute maximum of what
/// is behind the diode, against which `.clamping_voltage` is checked.
///
/// `.breakdown_voltage` is the minimum breakdown voltage VBR, an optional catalog column as for
/// `Tvs` (ADR 0073, ADR 0078).
///
/// The pins are `.a1` and `.a2`, the names and pads of KiCad 9's `Device:D_TVS` (described there
/// as a "Bidirectional transient-voltage-suppression diode", pins `A1` and `A2`, passive), and
/// `std/kicad.toml` maps the five packages of `Tvs` (`D_SOD-123`, `D_SOD-323`, `D_SMA`,
/// `D_SMB`, `D_SMC`, pads 1 and 2) to it. The catalog `tvs_bidirectional` has two rows, the
/// Littelfuse SMDJ12CA and SMDJ36CA, each read from the SMDJ series datasheet table (revised
/// 11/20/15) with the minimum breakdown voltage; `data/jlcpcb/README.md` records the sources.
/// More rows (the MDD SMAJ and SMBJ bidirectional types) need the same reading of their
/// datasheet.
///
/// Why a second generic and not a member of `Tvs` (ADR 0089). `std/kicad.toml` maps a kind and
/// a package to a symbol, so a member cannot choose the symbol: the mapping would need a
/// condition on a value, a change to the mapping's language. `Tvs`'s pins are `anode` and
/// `cathode`, which mislead for a part that has neither, and a pin table cannot differ by a
/// member. One catalog would hold rows of both kinds, and a design that did not say which would
/// be offered both. The name states the difference and sorts beside `Tvs`; `BidirectionalTvs`,
/// `TvsBi` and `TvsDual` (KiCad uses that for three-pin parts) were passed over, and renaming
/// `Tvs` to `TvsUnidirectional` would break every board that uses it. A design that wants a
/// bidirectional clamp writes `TvsBidirectional`; one that kept `Tvs` has the Zener symbol as
/// before. For a part across no rail a design states `.bias` as the largest voltage the line
/// can reach in either direction, a magnitude, so an asymmetric bidirectional part (a stand-off
/// that differs by polarity) has no field here. Three-pin parts (`D_TVS_Dual_*`) and multi-line
/// ESD arrays are not covered; each is its own part. The Littelfuse rows are Extended parts;
/// no Basic bidirectional row was read. The name, the pin names and the member-or-generic
/// choice are accepted as built (the owner, 2026-11-11).
generic TvsBidirectional {
    .designator &= "D"

    pins {
        .a1 passive "A1" "1"
        .a2 passive "A2" "2"
    }

    /// The reverse stand-off (working) voltage in either direction: the most the line may
    /// carry without the diode conducting.
    .standoff_voltage: Voltage
    /// The voltage across the diode while it carries `.peak_pulse_current`, in either
    /// direction.
    .clamping_voltage: Voltage
    .peak_pulse_current: Current
    /// The minimum breakdown voltage VBR in either direction, unset until a design states it
    /// or a catalog row does.
    .breakdown_voltage: Voltage
    /// The absolute maximum voltage of the part this one protects. A design states it.
    .protected_limit: Voltage
    /// The DC voltage across the diode, taken from the rail it is across whichever way round
    /// it is wired.
    .bias: Voltage

    .bias <= .standoff_voltage
    .clamping_voltage <= .protected_limit
    bridge .a1 ~> .a2

    .package: Package
    catalog tvs_bidirectional { .standoff_voltage, .clamping_voltage, .peak_pulse_current, .package, .breakdown_voltage optional }
}
